*.mph
*.png
*.fig
*.mat

# Simulation output in TEXT form. The binary patterns above already cover .mat/
# .png/.fig, which is precisely why only the .txt artifacts ever got committed -
# and they got committed under backslash-mangled names like
# ".\test\boomerang_bayesopt\...\DRYRUN_README.txt", produced when the old
# hard-coded '.\test\...' paths ran on macOS. On Windows a backslash IS a path
# separator, so git refuses to check such a name out at all: "error: invalid
# path". Every pattern here starts with * so it matches the mangled
# one-long-filename form as well as the proper nested one - * matches a
# backslash, since on POSIX that is an ordinary filename character.
*DRYRUN_README.txt
*bayesopt_boomerang*_log.txt
*optimization_*.txt

# and the whole output tree, when the paths are built correctly
omc-comsol-chang/test/

# COMSOL working files
*.mph.lock
*.mph.recovery*

# macOS
.DS_Store
omc-comsol-chang/test/
*bayesopt*log.txt

# Backslash-named artifacts: any script that still pastes a Windows separator
# into a path literal creates ONE file whose *name* contains backslashes when
# run on macOS/Linux. Committing such a file makes the repo impossible to check
# out on Windows ("error: invalid path"). This has regressed twice (1a4237f ->
# 8b1e7d4), so ignore the shape itself rather than the specific filenames.
*\\test\\*
*\\testing\\*
*\\optimize\\*

# Claude Code worktrees are real git checkouts living inside the working tree.
# Committed as gitlinks they are unfetchable (no .gitmodules, no URL) and show
# as permanently modified as their HEADs move.
.claude/worktrees/
